{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778554158196,"sessionID":"ses_1e5eb75c1ffeSiHOAuGDRfhx55","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b620ca4a3708d37e","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"chondrocyte"},"output":"Found 471 matches (showing first 100)\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__issue_context__.json:\n  Line 2:   \"body\": \"Please check that the term does not already exist by using the ontology search tool OLS:\\nhttps://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/cl\\n\\n\\n**Preferred term label**\\nfibrochondrocyte\\n\\n**Synonyms** (add reference(s), please)\\nmeniscus fibrochondrocyte - narrow synonym - PMID: [28939894](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28939894/)\\nFC - related synonym - abbreviation - PMID: [31871141](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31871141/)\\nfibrocartilage chondrocyte - exact synonym - PMID: 34608249\\n\\n**Definition** (free text, with reference(s), please. PubMed ID format is PMID:XXXXXX)\\nA chondrocyte with hybrid fibroblastic-chondrogenic characteristics found in fibrocartilage, particularly in the avascular inner zone and transitional middle zone of the meniscus (Wang et al., 2020).In humans, fibrochondrocytes are marked by predominant expression of type I collagen (COL1A1) and fibril-associated collagens (COL3A1, COL6A1) (Sun et al., 2019), while retaining type II collagen (COL2A1) expression and exhibiting lower SOX9 than hyaline chondrocytes (Liang et al., 2017).This molecular profile underlies the synthesis of abundant type I collagen essential for fibrocartilage matrix and reflects an intermediate phenotype between fibroblast and chondrocyte. \\n\\n**Parent cell type term** (check the hierarchy here https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/cl)\\n\\nConnective tissue cell \\nChondrocyte \\n\\n**Anatomical structure where the cell type is found** (check Uberon for anatomical structures: https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/uberon)\\nPart of fibrocartilage\\n\\nexpresses some 'collagen alpha-1(I) chain'\\n\\n**Your ORCID**\\nhttps://orcid.org/0009-0000-8480-9277\\n\\n**Additional notes or concerns**\\n\\n\",\n\n  Line 4:   \"title\": \"NTR - Fibrochondrocyte (FC)\",\n\n\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/patterns/definitions.owl:\n  Line 507: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"DOI:10.1038/cdd.2013.158\") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"DOI:10.1038/nri2395\") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"DOI:10.1038/s41536-019-0083-6\") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"DOI:10.3727/096368910X\") <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000134> \"Mesenchymal stem cells (MSCs) are multipotent stromal cells that can differentiate into a variety of cell types, including osteoblasts, chondrocytes, myocytes, and adipocytes. These cells originate mainly from the mesoderm of the embryo, which forms connective tissues, muscle, and the circulatory and urinary systems. However, in adults, MSCs are found in multiple tissues, including bone marrow, adipose tissue, the umbilical cord, and dental tissues. \n\n  Line 1566: Adipose-derived stem cells show immense promise in the field of regenerative medicine due to their ability to differentiate into adipocytes, chondrocytes, myocytes, osteoblasts, and other cell types. ADSCs also possess immunomodulatory and homeostatic functions: they have the ability to suppress immune responses and provide a therapeutic environment for tissue repair and regeneration, as well as supporting the proliferation of adipocytes and the overall expansion of adipose tissue and contributing to the tissue's ability to react to demands of energy storage and mobilization. Dysfunction of these cells may contribute to metabolic complications observed in obesity and diabetes. \n\n  Line 1735: Mesenchymal cells play a critical role in the development and maintenance of various biological systems throughout the body due to their versatility and adaptability. They can differentiate into more specialized cells, including osteoblasts, adipocytes, and chondrocytes, which are essential for bone, fat, and cartilage tissues, respectively. Furthermore, they contribute to the vascular and lymphatic systems through the formation of pericytes and lymphatic endothelial cells, securing the integrity of the circulatory system.\n\n\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/patterns/data/matches/cellPartOfAnatomicalEntity.tsv:\n  Line 38: http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_1001607\tarticular chondrocyte\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0010996\tarticular cartilage of joint\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000138\tchondrocyte\r\n\n  Line 201: http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_1000217\tgrowth plate cartilage chondrocyte\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0004129\tgrowth plate cartilage\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000138\tchondrocyte\r\n\n  Line 487: http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0019002\ttracheobronchial chondrocyte\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0007196\ttracheobronchial tree\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000138\tchondrocyte\r\n\n\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/patterns/data/default/ExtendedDescription.tsv:\n  Line 29: CL:0000134\tCL:0000134\tCL_0000134\t\"Mesenchymal stem cells (MSCs) are multipotent stromal cells that can differentiate into a variety of cell types, including osteoblasts, chondrocytes, myocytes, and adipocytes. These cells originate mainly from the mesoderm of the embryo, which forms connective tissues, muscle, and the circulatory and urinary systems. However, in adults, MSCs are found in multiple tissues, including bone marrow, adipose tissue, the umbilical cord, and dental tissues. \n\n  Line 741: Adipose-derived stem cells show immense promise in the field of regenerative medicine due to their ability to differentiate into adipocytes, chondrocytes, myocytes, osteoblasts, and other cell types. ADSCs also possess immunomodulatory and homeostatic functions: they have the ability to suppress immune responses and provide a therapeutic environment for tissue repair and regeneration, as well as supporting the proliferation of adipocytes and the overall expansion of adipose tissue and contributing to the tissue's ability to react to demands of energy storage and mobilization. Dysfunction of these cells may contribute to metabolic complications observed in obesity and diabetes. \n\n  Line 2075: Mesenchymal cells play a critical role in the development and maintenance of various biological systems throughout the body due to their versatility and adaptability. They can differentiate into more specialized cells, including osteoblasts, adipocytes, and chondrocytes, which are essential for bone, fat, and cartilage tissues, respectively. Furthermore, they contribute to the vascular and lymphatic systems through the formation of pericytes and lymphatic endothelial cells, securing the integrity of the circulatory system.\n\n\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/ontology/reports/summary_release.md:\n  Line 794: | UBERON:0001956 | cartilage of bronchus                                            | has part                                       | CL:0019002 | tracheobronchial chondrocyte                                                              |\n\n  Line 795: | UBERON:0001956 | cartilage of bronchus                                            | has_part                                       | CL:0019002 | tracheobronchial chondrocyte                                                              |\n\n  Line 980: | UBERON:0002242 | nucleus pulposus                                                 | has part                                       | CL:0000138 | chondrocyte                                                       